    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 46355,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Well, my experience, especially when I rough start, being raped at the age of 16, I dropped out of school at 17. And that's when my parents said, if you're going to stop going to school, you have to start going to work. And I took a job as a waitress at a waffle house on the side of the interstate. And I learned some very tough lessons during some very tough times in my life. And I learned about surviving. I learned about strength. I learned about having courage. something that was very difficult for a teenager going through this trauma that I had. And I'd eventually get my high school diploma. I'm gonna go to the Citadel. My father's a graduate of the Citadel. I used to serve in the Army for 28 years. My mom's a retired school teacher. And, you know, I went there to learn about myself more than anything. I wasn't going there to be the first woman to graduate from there, but I wanted to prove to myself I could face an obstacle, face a challenge, face adversity, and I wouldn't quit. And that determination, not knowing, now I'm 45, right? I'm, you know, about to go on a menopause. I've seen a lot now, but, you know, I'm in my mid 40s and I look back at my life and I just had no idea that this trauma The challenges that I had, the adversity I faced at the Citadel, it made me who I am today. It made me tough as nails. It made me be able to face these challenges head on no matter the headwinds. I've had so many, even in my first term in Congress. Both sides, right, just coming at me because I represent a swing district, but it's giving me strength and it's giving me and taught me courage, having the courage to speak up when it's just so important to the people that you represent. And there's nothing that beats the value of hard work. And all those experiences as tough as they were when I thought I would never make it have made me who I am. today and I'm grateful to have overcome that adversity to do what I'm doing today because having an independent voice in Congress right now at this time in our nation's history is more important than ever. You were the first",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I was raped at the age of 16.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "I dropped out of school at 17.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "My parents required me to work if I stopped attending school.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "I worked as a waitress at a Waffle House located on an interstate.",
        "confidence": 0.94
