  {
    "episode_id": 46355,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "What drove you? It's that inherent need for me, given the trauma, and I didn't know it at the time. I knew that there was something that I needed to do in my life by going to the Citadel. And that was facing a challenge and like anything else, facing an obstacle that I would take that obstacle on and I wouldn't quit. Because when I was raped and I had that trauma, I wanted to end everything. I quit school. I wanted to end my life. And I got a job. I got things together. I did what I needed to do to get through that experience as tough as it was. And it took a very long time. It's mental, it's physical, it's emotional healing. And that takes a lifetime. I'm still not recovered and fully healed from the experience, but it shapes who you are. And the Citadel for me was this opportunity to just say, F you to everybody in the world. I was angry. about what had happened to me and I wanted to go there and prove to myself that no matter what anyone threw at me that I could be successful. I could face that challenge head-on and I would not quit and that is a lesson that I learned. I learned too if I studied I could graduate top of my class, I taught myself to code when I was in college. I didn't know that I had that side of me, but it taught me so much about life, taught me about survival, taught me about strength, and it taught me about having the courage to do what is right, no matter the consequences.",
