    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Right now, I feel like I'm on an island. I'm literally a caucus of one. In our first week of votes, we had multiple abortion votes, and I'm pro-life. Okay, and I have a great pro-life voting record, but I also recognize that the environment has changed. This is not where the majority of Americans are. Neither side support the extremity of abortion up until birth. Well, that's just crazy. But just at the same way, advocating for a ban on all abortions with no exceptions. Well, that's crazy, too. And that's not where 80% to 90% of Americans are. And I represent a purple district, a swing district. And I have town halls almost every week that I'm home. I talk to my constituents. I poll on the issue. And right behind inflation during the midterm election, the number two issue, was abortion. And I talked to Republican women, Democrat women, independent women, and independents in my district, outnumber Republicans. And their voices aren't well-represented in Congress right now either. But I've been willing to have this conversation and say, where's the middle ground? And these people that are advocating for no exceptions, what are you talking about? What are you going to say to the 10-year-old girl who's raped by her father, her uncle, or someone else in her family? You know, and that's an extreme case, but those cases do happen. Or the young woman who has a fetus that has got so many abnormalities, if she were to carry it to harm, it could harm her life or harm her ability to get pregnant again in the future. What do you say to that young woman? And so, you know, these are uncomfortable conversations, but I've been a loud voice. I write up ads. I talk to constituents. I bring it up. And when we have these discussions, I often feel like I'm the only one in the room vocalizing what everybody else is feeling. Like I'm literally saying what's on everyone's minds, but they're afraid to say it because they're afraid of getting primary. They're afraid of the base. That's only a very small percentage, a handful of people, and a primary on",
