  {
    "episode_id": 46355,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and if you look at, everyone wants to fight. Everyone's fighting for the extremes of both sides right now, but this issue is affecting women every single day. We have entire counties and rural areas in South Carolina, like many other states that don't have a single OBGYN doctor. So, when you're talking about, hey, we want to save lives, what about giving access to every woman in the country to birth control? Last summer, only eight Republicans voted to protect women's access to birth control. And so, when you're talking about these issues, this is common sense, and I'm grateful for my governor in South Carolina, He signed into law legislation that would allow pharmacists to prescribe birth control pills to women over the counter. That is a great first step. There's much more to go here. What are you gonna do with the children who are born that don't have a home, the foster children, the orphans? I mean, what solution? We're not even having this conversation. It's just about fighting the other side because we wanna pay lip service to the pro-life movement. That's all you're doing. You're not actually doing anything to save real lives. And that's my problem with all of this. And I talked to Democrat women, I talked to Republicans, I talked to independents, somewhere in the middle. People are okay with gestational limits. Well, let's figure out what is that timeline. People are okay with exceptions for rape, incest, life of the mother, even fetal abnormality. So why can't we have that conversation? And I'm just sitting here alone ready to have it. But I'm the only one. It feels",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
