  {
    "episode_id": 46355,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "is one option, but first before we I go into the details of that, I want to reiterate that what you just said. This is we should put the blame of this on both parties. Republicans and Democrats alike for decades have been increasing spending with no in insight, and you have 49 out of 50 states that balance their budget every fiscal year. And they don't have a printing press to keep printing money like the Federal Reserve. And even California has figured out they balance their budget. The only state that doesn't is a state of Vermont. And so when you look at, for years we've had record revenues, the spending's been out of control. It's been this administration, the previous administration, the one, two, and three before that. The last time we had a budget and balanced it was under President Bill Clinton. The last Republican to balance the budget was Richard Nixon in 1970. What is clearly the process isn't working? And so utilizing this moment as an opportunity to say, hey, something's wrong here and we both sides need to take responsibility. accountability and put forth a plan. So the penny plan that Senator Rand Paul has filed in the Senate for a number of years, we've got the house version of it. When the penny plan was first created, you'd only have to cut a penny to balance the budget in about five years. Now we're up to six since the last year. I think it was five. Now we're up to six because we're at $31 trillion of debt. Now that's just to balance it in five years. Every year they're after the penny plan would allow for you to increase spending by double digits by over 10% every year they're after. We're going to file that bill again this session, but I'm even saying that do that now. I don't think you could do it realistically in five years. That's a significant amount of cuts. What I'm saying, I'm going to file that bill, but also I'm open to, let's look at a 10-year plan. I mean, I think that's the only way that you can get both Republicans and Democrats on board is to have a responsible plan that would do it in 10",
