    "turn_idx": 90,
    "turn_text": "Well, he was the only speaker possible for our conference because of the diversity of opinions, the diversity of political views within our conference. He's the only one that could bring us all together. Number one, number two, I don't take orders from anybody. I didn't when I was at the Citadel. I don't now. And I think that's very important that I keep true to who I am, which is very much trying to have an independent voice and being consistent on the Constitution. And on policies that matter to my state and my district, it's not going to be in no way is it going to be easy. And we just saw, I think it was this morning where our committee assignments came out and some of those holdouts. I mean, they got what they wanted. And there were handshakes. There were promises made. I don't know that we have all the answers, but some of the most vocal people got some of the most high-profile committee assignments. And I can appreciate having the diversity on all the committees, but when you reward those for that kind of behavior, then what kind of precedent does that set? What does that precedent does that set for the majority-makers, people that came in winning by one point? When I came into Congress, I flipped a seat that had a Democrat, and I won by one point. And so where are our views represented? And I think it's going to be incumbent upon us, those that have a more independent street to be more vocal, which is why, like right now, I'm looking around and it feels, it's very lonely right now. Because the reason that we're in the majority are because of people who have more of an independent street, those that flipped seats. That's the reason that we're",
