    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 136,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, they even run ads against me. Well, it is wildly popular even in conservative South Carolina. I would encourage all of my conservative colleagues to pull the issue in their districts and their states. And they will be overwhelmingly surprised. And I put forth the bill last session called the States Reform Act. And the conservative argument is to allow states to have the right to make their own laws on cannabis. And that's what the bill does. It provides a regulatory framework at the federal level. super low federal excise tax because we know that the higher the taxes are, the greater, the more illicit markets there are in that state. So you want to reduce the proclivity for that. But this has been a very long, very expensive, very painful, very harmful war on a plant. And I look at my own life, my own life experiences. When I was raped at the age of 16, I was in a really, really vulnerable position from a mental health perspective and getting through that. And I started self-medicating at the time, not realizing it because I was young. With cannabis, I cut my anxiety. I could sleep at night. I laughed. I told jokes. I thought I was funny. And I survived. And when you talk to people, whether it's a veteran who's got PTSD, someone with Parkinson's or a terminal illness, someone with epilepsy, and you see the miracles. that marijuana can bring to the lives of people. You have to ask yourself, where is the compassion? Why would we deprive people from a something that is not harmful, it is not a gateway drug, it is something that can save I heard you say",
