  {
    "episode_id": 46355,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46355.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 138,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it saved my life and you know I'm older now I can look back on why I did what I did and some of the decisions that I made I didn't know at the time I was self-medicating because you're I was just too close and it's too traumatic for the experience but when I look back at it now it's very very clear to me as an adult as a mother of two teenage kids that It literally, it changed my life in a way where I could move forward. I then went on, I did it for about a year, and I went on to the Citadel. I graduated in three years at top of my class as a first woman to do so. I worked in the corporate space for a while. I started my own company in 2008. I added on commercial real estate. Now I'm in Congress. I made it. And I've seen what it's done to people's lives and how miraculous it can be. And I don't know why any conservative or anyone would want to deprive another human from that opportunity. And that's the case that I've made. It can be conservative. It can be bipartisan. When you allow people, you allow states to be in their driver's seat on this issue and make their own laws, either to have it or prohibit it. I mean, it's wildly popular with conservatives, even across the state of South Carolina. It's a no-brainer. What do you say to the conservatives who disagree with you? Well, the data is there. You can look at the Journal of American Medicine, their study after study. We need more studies, I would argue as well, but it's not mental health issues or a gateway drug to heavier drugs. Prescription opioids, prescription painkillers, being prescribed heroin for your pain, is a gateway drug. And you talk to police officers. They're not arresting people that are, you know, that are on marijuana or on cannabis for violent crimes. That just is not a thing. And so, you know, looking at the real causes here, that's where we have to be as opposed to playing to a very small, narrow, single-digit margin of your base in a primary. And we're talking about a very small group of folks that believe it's a gateway drug. The vast majority of people just aren't there. And a lot has changed over the last 80 years. And so even talking to my parents, they've changed. They're in their 80s now, but they are much more open to it. And then when you pull it and you see how popular it is, but federalism is inherent to our nation. And that's why I sponsored a bill, drafted a bill, giving states the right to determine their own cannabis laws. It's just common sense. This is a",
